laurel 3000: LAUREL AUDIO lead: the speaker stream queues 3 periods of 20 ms at the DSP (was 8) and takes frames from the ring only once it holds 30 ms; the amp goes off 2 s after the last sound, the DSP path 12 s after it (was 2 s, with a full bring-up at the next sound); both kernels. docs/laurel_sprout/audio.md "Lead".

[V 2026-11-03, logs/usblog-20261103-071332.txt, kernel 48e56e9] Three speaker streams: 24 underruns of 164 periods, 51 of 319, 83 of 670 (a partial period
= zeros for the rest of it, and the sound moves later by that much). [I from the code] With 8 periods queued from the first write, the ring was drained at the
start: period 0 held the sound's first frames and periods 1..7 silence, so the second chunk of every sound came 140 ms after the first; in steady state the
ring held about one HAL I/O buffer and the refill raced the HAL's write. The HAL's clock is mach_absolute_time (audio_plugin.c get_zero_time_stamp), the DSP's
is the I2S bit clock: nothing tied the ring level to a lead. Now: IN_FLIGHT 3, LEAD_FRAMES 1440, LEAD_MAX_MS 100 (a thin writer), a dry ring waits for the lead
again (one gap, counted as "lead waits"), QUIET_MS 12000, AMP_QUIET_MS 2000. The "stream: playing" line now says how long path_start() took and how long
after the first sound frames it was. Kill switch laurel_audio_lead=0 (8 periods, no lead, path off after 2 s). The self-check (path_check) is unchanged in
what it proves: 2 periods back in 500 ms.

